      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nEvery product organization builds roadmaps, yet most end up abandoned, ignored, or quietly rewritten into irrelevance. The failure is rarely about effort or intelligence. It is structural: roadmaps are asked to be a plan, a promise, and a persuasion tool at once — and those roles conflict.\n\nFirst, most roadmaps confuse outputs with outcomes. They are lists of features with dates attached — \"launch X in Q2\" — with the \"why\" missing. Which customer problem does X solve? Which metric will it move? Without stated outcomes, teams become feature factories, optimizing for shipping rather than value, and the roadmap can only be judged by whether things shipped on time, not whether they mattered.\n\nSecond, they offer false precision. Attaching dates to unexplored work implies a certainty that product development never has. Building software is an exercise in learning, and learning resists scheduling. When reality diverges — an assumption fails, discovery reveals a better solution, a dependency slips — the dated roadmap becomes a ledger of broken promises rather than a planning tool. The dates convert hypotheses into commitments.\n\nThird, many roadmaps are political artifacts rather than strategic ones. They emerge from negotiation among stakeholders: sales needs a feature to close a deal, an executive wants a box checked from the vision deck, engineering lobbies for debt paydown. The result is a compromise that satisfies everyone individually and serves the customer not at all. Evidence — usage data, customer interviews, market signals — is conspicuously absent from the process.\n\nFourth, a single roadmap cannot serve every audience. Leadership wants strategy; sales wants something sellable; engineering wants a plan; customers want commitments. Pressed into all these roles, the document fails at each. The most damaging version is when sales treats the roadmap as a catalog and sells future features to customers, at which point flexibility dies. The roadmap is now a contract, and every reprioritization is a breach.\n\nWhat would a better roadmap look like? It would express problems and goals rather than solutions: \"reduce onboarding drop-off,\" not \"launch onboarding wizard.\" It would use horizons — now, next, later — instead of dates, staying honest about uncertainty. It would be a living document, revised as learning accumulates rather than defended as it ages. And it would distinguish clearly between what is committed and what is merely being explored.\n\nThe deepest issue is that most organizations treat the roadmap as a promise to keep when it should be a hypothesis to test. Plans fail when they pretend the future is known. Roadmaps survive only when they admit what they are: a statement of direction, grounded in evidence, flexible enough to change when the world does. The roadmap doesn't fail because plans changed; it fails because it pretended they wouldn't.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to connect strategy with execution. Yet many become unreliable lists of features, missed deadlines, and outdated assumptions. They fail not because planning is useless, but because organizations often expect roadmaps to provide certainty in an environment defined by uncertainty.\n\nThe first problem is that many roadmaps are not strategic. Instead of expressing customer problems, business outcomes, and important choices, they become inventories of stakeholder requests. Sales wants one feature, executives want another, and competitors inspire several more. The resulting roadmap may satisfy internal politics, but it offers no coherent explanation of how the product will create value.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they are treated as fixed commitments. Teams are asked to predict, months in advance, exactly what they will build and when they will deliver it. But product development involves discovery. Customer needs, technical constraints, market conditions, and regulatory requirements can change quickly. A roadmap that cannot adapt to new evidence forces teams either to build the wrong thing or to appear unreliable when plans change.\n\nAnother common failure is confusing outputs with outcomes. Shipping a dashboard or launching an integration is an output; improving retention, reducing customer effort, or increasing conversion is an outcome. Feature-based roadmaps encourage teams to measure progress by delivery rather than impact. This can create a productive-looking organization that releases frequently without solving meaningful problems.\n\nPoor prioritization makes matters worse. When every request is labeled important, teams become overloaded and fragmented. Frequent changes in direction increase context switching, create technical debt, and prevent initiatives from receiving enough attention to succeed. A roadmap without explicit trade-offs is not a roadmap—it is a wish list.\n\nMany roadmaps also ignore capacity and dependencies. They assume ideal staffing, underestimate maintenance work, and overlook research, testing, infrastructure, security, and coordination with other teams. Dates then become detached from operational reality. Once a few items slip, confidence in the entire plan collapses.\n\nFinally, roadmaps often fail as communication tools. Different audiences interpret them differently: executives see commitments, sales teams see promises, and product teams see hypotheses. Without clear language about confidence, time horizons, and decision criteria, the roadmap creates misalignment rather than clarity.\n\nEffective roadmaps are living strategic documents. They focus on problems and outcomes, distinguish near-term commitments from longer-term possibilities, and make priorities and trade-offs visible. They are reviewed as evidence changes and supported by measurable goals. The best roadmap does not pretend to predict the future. It helps an organization navigate it.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
